.TH CRUNCH 1 "" "crunch %VERSION%" "Crunch Manual"
.SH NAME
crunch \- compiler driver for the Crunch language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B crunch
.RI [ options ]
.IR input\-file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B crunch
compiles Crunch source files, assembles GNU assembly files, and links object
files for Linux x86\-64.
It runs the compilation stages as a pipeline:
the preprocessor
.BR crunch\-pp ,
the IR generator
.BR crunch\-ir ,
the code generator
.BR crunch\-asm ,
the assembler and finally the linker. The assembler defaults to GNU as when
detected by configure, otherwise crunch-as. Dynamic executables use crunch-ld;
static and relocatable links use the configured linker, defaulting to GNU ld
when detected and crunch-ld otherwise.
--enable-native-only selects crunch-as and crunch-ld without requiring GNU
binutils.
The experimental
.B crunch\-as
object assembler can be selected with
.BR \-\-with\-crunch\-as ;
it does not yet support all generated assembly and does not fall back to GNU
as on failure. The runtime library is built with crunch-as by default; configure
with
.B \-\-with\-runtime\-assembler=gnu
to use GNU as instead.
The
.B \-\-help
output shows the configured assembler and archiver, and the linkers used for
dynamic executables and for static or relocatable links.
The Crunch stage tools are looked up in the directory of the
.B crunch
executable.
GNU as and GNU ld are the normal defaults. Configure can select crunch-ld as
the default linker or both native tools in native-only mode; the configuration
file can override the tool selections and executable paths.
The experimental crunch-ld supports relocatable linking
.RB ( \-c
or assembly output to
.BR .o )
including section groups, but rejects some options and section types. It does not
fall back to GNU ld on failure.
Each Crunch source is preprocessed independently. Files ending in
.B .s
are assembled independently without preprocessing. Files ending in
.B .S
are preprocessed with Crunch's macro and include rules, then assembled.
The
.B \-I
option supplies search directories for assembler
.B .include
directives in both
.B .s
and
.B .S
inputs. The assembler searches the working directory first, followed by the
specified directories in order. The
.B \-I
and
.B \-D
options also apply to preprocessing
.B .S
inputs. Assembly preprocessing defines
.B __ASSEMBLER__
to 1 unless supplied with
.BR \-D .
At the start of a line, recognized preprocessor directives take precedence
over assembly comments; other
.B #
comments are preserved without macro expansion.
.PP
The unsupported directives
.BR #error ,
.BR #warning ,
.BR #line ,
and
.B #pragma
are rejected in active sections.
.PP
Files ending in
.B .o
are passed to the linker as objects, and files ending in
.B .a
are passed as static archives. Inputs are passed in their original order.
With
.BR \-\-dynamic ,
shared objects named
.B .so
or
.B .so.N
may also be supplied directly.
Both supported linkers search archives when encountered; place an archive
after the inputs that reference it, or repeat it when needed. A relocatable
link retains unresolved references for a later link.
The driver can create a static archive from object files using
.BR \-\-archive .
The
.B \-l
option selects a library from the
.B \-L
and configured search directories.
These inputs may be mixed.
If at least one input ends in
.B .s
or
.B .S
and the explicit output name ends in
.BR .o ,
a combined relocatable object is produced, as with
.BR \-c .
Without a
.B .s
or
.B .S
input, an
.B .o
output name does not select object mode.
Object and executable links use a private temporary directory beside the output,
which is replaced only after a successful link. A failed link preserves an
existing output. Named regular-file outputs from
.BR \-E ,
.BR \-\-emit\-ir ,
and
.B \-S
are staged in the same way, preserving existing read, write, and execute
permission bits. The output directory must be writable. On success, replacement
breaks hard links and replaces an output symlink rather than following it;
the former target is unchanged. Standard output and intermediate outputs
directed to special files remain streams and cannot be rolled back on failure.
Executables require a globally defined
.BR _start ,
generated by Crunch for
.B main
or supplied by an input object or assembly file.
Multiple inputs and
.B \-c
use separate compilation.
Single-Crunch-source executable builds,
.BR \-\-emit\-ir ,
and
.B \-S
use the executable-oriented mode, which requires a defined
.BR main .
.B \-E
only preprocesses the source and does not require
.BR main .
The
.B \-E
mode accepts a single Crunch or
.B .S
source. The
.BR \-\-emit\-ir
and
.B \-S
modes accept a single Crunch source.
File-scope external scalar, supported pointer, and dynamic-array declarations
are supported; external structure objects are not. External dynamic-array
declarations cannot specify a size or initializer.
Fixed arrays are recognized and checked by the frontend, but IR generation
rejects programs containing them until fixed-array storage is implemented.
Use explicit
.B dim
declarations for initialized or sized dynamic arrays.
Runtime global-array initializers run once before Crunch
.B main
or through standard C runtime startup. A custom assembly entry point must call
.B __crunch_run_initializers
before using initialized globals. Initializers within each object run in
declaration order; object order follows the linked object order.
Each function and external variable must have a compatible declaration in each
translation unit that uses it; use shared headers because the linker does not
check types.
.PP
In separate compilation, a normal prototype for a recognized Crunch library
function selects the library when no definition is present in that translation
unit. Use an explicit
.B extern
function declaration to select an ordinary external function with the same
name instead. An unresolved explicit
.B extern
function requires separate compilation. File-scope
.B static
functions and variables have object-local linkage. File-scope variable
declarations may be grouped as
.BR "static { ... };" ,
or as
.B static const { ... };
to apply both qualifiers.
.PP
The reserved word
.B typedef
declares a scoped type alias, for example
.BR "typedef int Count;" .
Scalar, supported pointer, and named-structure types can be aliased; array and
function-type aliases are not supported.
.PP
The
.BR <fcntl.h> ,
.BR <unistd.h> ,
.BR <sys/stat.h> ,
.BR <stdio.h> ,
and
.B <dirent.h>
headers declare the Linux file routines
.BR open ,
.BR openat ,
.BR fstatat ,
.BR readlinkat ,
.BR unlinkat ,
.BR mkdirat ,
.BR renameat ,
.BR symlinkat ,
.BR read ,
.BR write ,
.BR lseek ,
.BR close ,
.BR stat ,
.BR fstat ,
.BR lstat ,
.BR readlink ,
.BR getdents64 ,
.BR dirent64_next ,
.BR unlink ,
.BR rename ,
.BR mkdir ,
and
.BR rmdir ,
as well as the existing
.B file_*
routines. Failures return -1 and set the process-wide
.B errno
declared in
.BR <errno.h> .
The three-argument
.B open
uses Linux flags and creation permissions;
.B stat
uses the Linux x86-64 structure layout. See the language specification for
the full contracts.
.PP
The
.B <stdlib.h>
header declares
.BR malloc ,
.BR realloc ,
and
.BR free
for explicitly managed memory. A failed nonzero resize leaves the original
allocation valid; a zero-size resize of a nonnull pointer releases it.
Do not mix these allocations with
.BR dim ,
.BR munmap ,
or a different allocator. See
.BR malloc (3crunch),
.BR realloc (3crunch),
and
.BR free (3crunch).
.PP
The
.B **
operator raises a numeric base to a numeric power and returns a
.BR real .
It is right-associative, binds more tightly than unary plus or minus, and
supports negative and fractional exponents. Write multiplication by a
dereferenced pointer with a space, as in
.BR "a * *p" .
See the language specification for numerical behavior and global initializer
restrictions.
.PP
The input name
.B \-
reads one source file from standard input; it may appear at most once.
.PP
By default
.B crunch
produces an executable named
.BR a.out .
The options below stop the pipeline at an earlier stage.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-c11\-numeric
Accepted for compatibility; C-style numeric promotions and conversions are
now the default. Plain char remains unsigned. Integer types and other language
rules are not yet fully C11-compatible.
.TP
.BI \-L " dir"
Add a directory to the link-time library search path. All such directories are
searched first, in their command-line order.
.TP
.BI \-l " name"
Link a library at this position in the input order. For dynamic executables,
search each directory for lib\fIname\fR.so before lib\fIname\fR.a.
.B \-l:FILE.a
selects an exact archive filename; dynamic mode also accepts exact
.B \-l:FILE.so
and
.B \-l:FILE.so.N
names. Static and relocatable links search only archives. Additional shared
dependencies must be available to the loader at runtime; \-L does not set a
runtime search path. Shared libraries without a SONAME are recorded by filename.
.TP
.B \-\-archive
Create a fresh indexed static archive from one or more
.B .o
inputs. Requires
.BR \-o ;
duplicate member basenames are rejected. The output is replaced only on
success. Uses the configure-selected GNU ar or crunch-ar unless
.B archiver_path
is configured.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic
Explicitly select the default dynamic executable mode using crunch-ld and the
shared Crunch runtime.
This overrides a GNU linker selection; a configured linker_path is used when
linker = crunch. The runtime directory is embedded as an absolute RUNPATH and
must remain available at execution time. Build-tree executables use the build
directory; installed executables use the configured Crunch library directory.
Directories containing ':' or '$' are rejected. Direct shared-object inputs
are accepted, and \-l may select shared libraries. Other shared
dependencies need their own loader search paths. Dynamic output has restricted
relocation support and does not run shared-library finalizers on Crunch exits.
The executable may not define its own global errno or directly link another
shared library that defines it, which could conflict with the protected
runtime storage. Transitive dependencies are not checked. Older objects and assembly with direct
PC-relative references to shared data require GOT relocations or \-\-static.
See the crunch-ld README for limitations.
.TP
.B \-\-static
Select static executable mode. Neither this option nor
\-\-dynamic can be combined with archive or intermediate-output modes, or
with each other.
.TP
.B \-\-glibc
Compile modules without a Crunch entry point and link with the system C
compiler's glibc startup and libraries. Crunch's built-in include directory
is omitted; set CRUNCH_INCLUDE_PATH to the system header directories and use
\-I for project headers. Select this option for both compilation and linking
of separate objects. The driver adds the compiler support archive, not the
Crunch libc. This experimental mode produces non-PIE dynamic executables and
cannot be combined with \-\-dynamic, \-\-static, \-\-pie, \-z, \-\-rpath, or
\-\-archive. Not all GNU language extensions are supported.
.TP
.B \-Wabi\-mismatch
Warn when available Crunch IR ABI records for declarations differ from
selected object definitions. This best-effort check includes extracted
archive members and visible candidates in directly linked shared libraries,
but not missing metadata, indirect dependencies, or later-loaded libraries.
Runtime symbol interposition is not fully modeled.
It may warn about unused declarations or compatible calling conventions.
Static and relocatable links need the configured crunch linker; dynamic
executable links use it by default. If GNU ld is selected, the driver warns
that ABI checking is discarded and continues. Warnings do not fail the link.
.TP
.B \-Wunused\-variable
Warn about unused local variables; parameters are exempt. Disabled by default.
.TP
.B \-Wcase\-fall\-through
Warn about switch case fall-through. Disabled by default.
.TP
.B \-Wall
Enable all optional warning options, including
.B \-Wunused\-variable
and
.B \-Wcase\-fall\-through
and
.BR \-Wabi\-mismatch .
.TP
.B \-pie, \-\-pie
Produce a position-independent dynamic executable. The default executable
remains fixed-address. Inputs must use supported position-independent
relocations; dynamic text relocations, narrow absolute image addresses, and
direct PC-relative references to shared data are rejected. This option is
invalid for static, archive, intermediate, or relocatable output.
.TP
.B \-\-rpath PATH, \-rpath PATH
Add a runtime search path to a dynamic executable. The forms
\-\-rpath=PATH and \-rpath=PATH are also supported. Repeated paths precede
the driver's built-in runtime directory in command-line order. Quote
'$ORIGIN' to preserve it for the loader. Explicit paths can select a different
shared runtime before the built-in fallback, which still points to the original
build or installation. RUNPATH is used for direct dependencies, not their
children, and does not affect link-time library search. Empty path arguments
are rejected; empty colon-separated components can search the current working
directory. This option is invalid outside dynamic executable links.
.TP
.B \-z relro, \-zrelro
Opt into GNU RELRO for a dynamic executable. The loader makes .dynamic,
the GOT, initialization and finalization arrays, and supported .data.rel.ro
sections read-only after relocation; ordinary data and BSS remain writable.
Dynamic bindings are eager even without RELRO. RELRO is disabled by default
and is invalid for static, archive, intermediate, or relocatable output.
.TP
.B \-\-with\-crunch\-as
Use the experimental Crunch object assembler beside the driver instead of
the configured assembler for this invocation. Configure's
.B \-\-with\-default\-assembler=gnu|crunch
sets the default; configure's
.B \-\-with\-gnu\-as=FILE
sets the GNU assembler path.
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Write the output to
.IR file .
The name
.B \-
means standard output, which is allowed only for
.BR \-E ,
.BR \-\-emit\-ir ,
and
.BR \-S .
.TP
.BI \-I " dir"
Add
.I dir
to the header and assembler include search paths.
For preprocessor headers, except for quoted includes searched relative to the
including file, directories given with
.B \-I
are searched first and may be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-D " name[=value]"
Define a preprocessor macro before the source is read.
.B \-D name
behaves like
.B #define name
(usable with
.BR #ifdef );
.B \-D name=value
behaves like
.BR "#define name value"
or
.BR "#macro name value" .
May be repeated; redefining an existing macro is an error.
.PP
In source files,
.B #define name value
is equivalent to
.BR "#macro name value" ;
both also support function\-like macros. A valueless
.B #define name
defines a non\-expanding flag suitable for include guards. A backslash
immediately followed by a newline continues a
.B #define
or
.B #macro
definition without inserting whitespace. Expression\-based
.B #if
and
.B #elif
support macro expansion, integer expressions, and
.B defined
tests. Unknown identifiers and non\-expanding flags evaluate to zero;
use
.B defined
to test whether a flag exists.
.TP
.B \-E
Stop after preprocessing and write the preprocessed source to standard output
unless
.B \-o
is given. Only one source file is accepted.
.TP
.B \-\-emit\-ir
Stop after generating Crunch IR and write it to standard output unless
.B \-o
is given. Only one source file is accepted.
.TP
.B \-S
Stop after generating GNU assembly and write it to standard output unless
.B \-o
is given. Only one source file is accepted. The output records the source
basename with a .file directive and emits string literals with .string.
.TP
.B \-c
Produce one relocatable object file, combining multiple inputs if given.
The default output name is
.BR a.o ;
in
.B \-\-glibc
mode, a single named source with an extension defaults to its basename with a
.B .o
suffix.
.TP
.B \-v
Print the command run for each stage to standard error.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version, the date and time the driver was compiled, and the
operating system name and machine architecture, then exit.
.SH HEADER SEARCH ORDER
An
.B #include <name>
directive is resolved by searching, in order:
.IP 1. 3
the directories given with
.BR \-I ;
.IP 2. 3
the colon\-separated directories in
.BR CRUNCH_INCLUDE_PATH ;
.IP 3. 3
the built\-in header directory, %INCLUDEDIR%;
.IP 4. 3
the directories listed by
.B include_path
in the configuration file.
.PP
An
.B #include "name"
directive (a user header) first looks in the directory of the file that
contains the directive, so
.B #include "inc/some_header.h"
works without any option.
If the file is not there, the search order above is used.
Absolute names and names containing
.B ..
components are rejected.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
The driver and preprocessor read %CONFFILE% if it exists.
Blank lines are ignored and
.B #
starts a comment.
.BI include_path " = " dir\fR[\fB:\fIdir\fR...]\fR;
whitespace around
.B =
is allowed and repeated lines append directories.
The
.B assembler
and
.B linker
keys accept
.B gnu
or
.BR crunch .
Their last occurrences override the configured defaults.
The
.B library_path
key specifies colon-separated library search directories; repeated
lines append entries. These follow all
.B \-L
directories and replace the compiled-in default of PREFIX/lib. If the key is
absent, that default is used. It does not change the runtime library location.
The optional
.BR assembler_path ,
.BR linker_path ,
and
.B archiver_path
keys select absolute executable paths; the last occurrence wins. Paths are
used literally and may contain spaces. The assembler and linker selections
still determine the tool interface; use
.B linker = crunch
with crunch-ld or its wrapper. The archiver must accept the
.B ar rcs
interface.
.B \-\-with\-crunch\-as
overrides both the assembler selection and path.
An unknown key, a missing
.BR = ,
an invalid tool selection, a non-absolute tool path, or an empty directory
entry is an error.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CRUNCH_INCLUDE_PATH
Colon\-separated list of header directories, searched after
.B \-I
directories.
A leading
.B ~
is not expanded.
.TP
.B CRUNCH_CONF_FILE
Use this configuration file instead of the compiled\-in one.
.TP
.B CRUNCH_GLIBC_CC
Absolute path of the system C linker driver used by \-\-glibc.
The default is /usr/bin/cc.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Directory for temporary object files made while compiling.
The default is
.BR /tmp .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I libcrunch.a
Static standard library searched by the linker; only referenced functions are
included in the executable. Installed in the configured library directory.
.TP
.I libcrunch-support.a
Compiler helper archive linked in glibc mode, without the Crunch libc.
.TP
.I %CONFFILE%
Default configuration file.
.TP
.I %INCLUDEDIR%
Directory with the standard headers
.IR string.h ,
.IR stdio.h ,
.IR stdlib.h ,
.IR unistd.h ,
.IR fcntl.h ,
.IR sys/stat.h ,
.IR sys/mman.h ,
.IR environment.h ,
and
.IR errno.h .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success,
.B 1
if a compilation stage, the assembler, or the linker fails or an output file
cannot be written, and
.B 2
for invalid command\-line usage.
.SH EXAMPLES
Compile and run a program:
.PP
.RS
.nf
crunch hello.bc \-o hello
\&./hello
.fi
.RE
.PP
Compile and link separate files:
.PP
.RS
.nf
crunch \-o program main.c helper.c
crunch \-c \-o combined.o main.c helper.c
crunch \-o program combined.o
crunch \-o assembly.o start.s helper.s
crunch \-o program assembly.o
crunch \-o program main.bc helper.s
crunch \-I headers \-DVALUE=42 \-o program main.bc helper.S
.fi
.RE
.PP
Inspect the generated assembly:
.PP
.RS
.nf
crunch \-S hello.bc
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR input (3),
.BR as (1),
.BR ld (1)
